<header><p class="st-kicker">prince bishopric · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Prince-Bishopric of Passau</h1><p class="st-meta">1217 CE – 1803 CE · capital Passau</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Danube church state at the meeting of three rivers, whose vast diocese once stretched to Vienna and whose court may have produced the Nibelungenlied.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-boniface">Saint Boniface</a> organised the diocese of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/passau">Passau</a> in 739. As the Bavarian church expanded eastwards, its diocese came to cover much of today's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>. In 1217 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-ii">Emperor Frederick II</a> recognised the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a>'s secular lordship over the city and nearby lands, making Passau a prince-bishopric directly under the Empire.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The cathedral chapter elected the prince-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a>, who ruled a small territory around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/passau">Passau</a> and in the Bavarian Forest, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salt-trade">salt trade</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inn-river">Inn</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nibelungenlied">Nibelungenlied</a> may have been composed around 1200 at the court of Bishop <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wolfger-von-erla">Wolfger von Erla</a>. Conflicts with the citizens recurred. After a fire in 1662 Italian architects rebuilt the city and St. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/st-stephens-cathedral-passau">Stephen's Cathedral</a> in baroque style.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The prince-bishopric was secularised in 1803 and divided between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a> and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/electorate-of-salzburg">Electorate of Salzburg</a>; by 1805 all of it belonged to Bavaria. The Catholic diocese of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/passau">Passau</a> continues, and the baroque old town still clusters on the narrow peninsula between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inn-river">Inn</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/prince-bishopric-of-passau.jpg" alt="Prince-Bishopric of Passau" width="320" height="232"><figcaption>Image: Aconcagua · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Passauer_Dom.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1217 CE – 1803 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>prince bishopric</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>theocratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Passau</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Wolfger von Erla, Leopold V of Austria</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholicism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Bavarian German</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>, <a href="/countries/austria">Austria</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Hochstift Passau</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1510&amp;focus=prince-bishopric-of-passau" aria-label="Prince-Bishopric of Passau on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4992 480 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Prince-Bishopric of Passau lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5312 736L5229 694M5312 736L5269 749"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5229 694h0M5269 749h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5312 736h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5312 736h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Passau. <a href="/atlas?year=1510&amp;focus=prince-bishopric-of-passau">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
